      "code_sha256_lf": "b6feab70e5d4ae9f688bd83a2f6e406817e94c68871122ef5ed5c9c1365c098a",
      "rationale": "sqlparse.filters.tokens.IdentifierCaseFilter.process has its quote check inverted: `if ... and value.strip()[0] == '\"': value = self.convert(value)` means it changes the case of double-quoted identifiers (T.Name / T.String.Symbol tokens) and leaves unquoted ones untouched. This is backwards from the library's documented intent: the CHANGELOG explicitly records \"Ignore identifier in double-quotes when changing cases (issue 21)\", i.e. identifier_case must fold the case of ordinary (unquoted) identifiers while leaving quoted identifiers exactly as written, since SQL quoting preserves case. The three failing checks reported (\"format: identifiercase\", \"format: identifiercase quotes\", \"issue469 copy as psql command\") all exercise exactly this single code path with different statements (plain SELECT, SELECT with a quoted identifier, and a COPY/psql-style command with a quoted identifier), so one test per scenario reproduces the whole regression. The test asserts the documented behavior (unquoted name upper-cased, quoted names left verbatim) which fails on the current inverted condition and will pass once the `==` is corrected to the intended \"not quoted\" check.",
